{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgimRfBxSfAMmTaUCPen5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WoB55dnxxxDWn15zpKQbup","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with the clef, the key signature and \"fe:\". Bar 11 comes first. It has a high C6 eighth, then the 32nd figure A5-G5-A5-Bb5 and C6 again, then an eighth rest. After \"po.\" come four 16ths, each followed by a 16th rest: C5, Bb4, A4 and a high C6. That matches bar 11 exactly. A bar line follows, then \"fe:\" again for bar 12. It has a Bb5 eighth, the 32nd figure G5-F5-G5-A5 and Bb5 again, then an eighth rest. After \"po:\" come Bb4, A4, G4 and a high Bb5, each followed by a 16th rest. That matches bar 12. After the next bar line there are four beamed groups of four 32nds. The first two sit high (A5 F5 E5 F5, G5 F5 E5 F5). The next two sit lower (E5 Bb4 A4 Bb4, C5 Bb4 A4 Bb4). That is the first half of bar 13. The line ends with the half-bar mark and a slur running toward the next line. So bar 13 is split, and the next line finishes it. Nothing contradicts the edition.","right":true,"insights":"The line was easy to match. The forte/piano alternation and the rest-separated 16ths on a falling line make bars 11 and 12 unmistakable. The only harder call was where the line stops: bars 13–15 repeat the same 32nd figure, so the end needed counting 32nd groups to see that it stops halfway through bar 13. A clearer last group or the start of the next line would have settled that faster."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5851,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5851},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"